Toni Braxton's third studio album, *The Heat*, released on April 25, 2000, marked a departure from her signature ballads, embracing a more urban sound. This shift is evident in tracks like "He Wasn't Man Enough," which features a synth-funk bassline and taut beats, showcasing Braxton's exploration of contemporary R&B styles. ([en.wikipedia.org](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Heat_%28Toni_Braxton_album%29?utm_source=openai))

The lead single from *The Heat*, "He Wasn't Man Enough," was released on March 17, 2000. The song achieved critical acclaim, winning a Grammy Award for Best Female R&B Vocal Performance and reaching number two on the Billboard Hot 100 chart. ([en.wikipedia.org](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Heat_%28Toni_Braxton_album%29?utm_source=openai))

With *The Heat*, LaFace Records aimed to reposition Braxton as a more contemporary R&B artist, moving away from her previous adult contemporary image. This strategic shift was intended to appeal to a younger audience and align her with the evolving music landscape of the early 2000s. ([en.wikipedia.org](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Heat_%28Toni_Braxton_album%29?utm_source=openai))  No source extracted due to invalid syntax

